    "content": "---\nid: usda_cfp_food_access\ntitle: \"USDA Community Food Projects (CFP) Competitive Grant\"\nfunder: \"USDA National Institute of Food and Agriculture (NIFA)\"\nprogram: \"Community Food Projects Competitive Grant Program (CFP)\"\namount: 250000\namountFormatted: \"$250,000\"\ndeadline: \"2026-11-30\"\ndeadlineFormatted: \"November 30, 2026\"\ntier: \"Priority 1 (USDA Food Access)\"\ncategory: \"Federal/USDA\"\nmatchPercentage: 50\ngrantType: \"Federal Competitive Grant\"\nportalUrl: \"https://grants.gov\"\nstrategicPriority: \"Resident-Owned Food Retail & Fresh Produce Access\"\nstatus: \"Planned\"\n---\n\n# USDA Community Food Projects (CFP) Competitive Grant\n\n**Awarding Agency:** USDA National Institute of Food and Agriculture (NIFA)  \n**Program:** Community Food Projects (CFP) Competitive Grants Program  \n**Funding Request:** $250,000 (3-Year Period)  \n**Statutory Match Requirement:** 50% ($125,000 Non-Federal Match via Philanthropy / In-Kind)  \n**Applicant Entity:** Go Austin / Vamos Austin (GAVA)  \n**Target Geography:** Eastern Crescent of Austin (Dove Springs, Montopolis, Colony Park)  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Project Abstract & Mission Alignment\nGo Austin / Vamos Austin (GAVA) requests $250,000 under the USDA Community Food Projects (CFP) program to launch and scale a resident-governed, community-owned cooperative grocery and food hub in Southeast Austin's Eastern Crescent. This geography is designated by the USDA Economic Research Service as a severe low-income, low-access food apartheid zone. This initiative pairs local agricultural sourcing (at least 30% from regional Texas BIPOC producers) with a democratic, multicultural co-ownership governance structure (at least 51% community resident leadership on the governing board).\n\n## 2. Statement of Community Need\nOver 45,000 residents in Dove Springs and Montopolis reside more than 2.5 miles from a full-service grocery store, relying on discount dollar stores and convenience stations. Rates of diet-related chronic diseases (Type 2 diabetes, childhood hypertension) in target census tracts exceed Austin city averages by 2.4x.\n\n## 3. Scope of Work & Milestone Schedule\n- **Q4 2026:** Finalize co-op legal incorporation, bylaws, and community steering committee governance.\n- **Q1–Q2 2027:** Storefront site lease acquisition, refrigeration fixture installation, and POS deployment.\n- **Q3–Q4 2027:** Grand opening of retail food co-op; kickoff of bilingual nutrition and SNAP incentive campaigns.\n- **2028–2029:** Scale wholesale partnerships with regional food hubs and achieve financial operational break-even.\n\n## 4. Comprehensive Budget Outline\n| Line Item | Description | Federal Request | Match Source | Total Budget |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Personnel** | 1.0 FTE Project Director & 0.5 FTE Bilingual Community Organizer | $130,000 | $40,000 (Philanthropic) | $170,000 |\n| **Equipment** | Commercial cold-storage, display cases, POS inventory system | $70,000 | $60,000 (Foundation Grants) | $130,000 |\n| **Supplies & Marketing**| Bilingual outreach materials, signage, community workshops | $25,000 | $15,000 (In-kind Volunteer) | $40,000 |\n| **Contractual** | Financial modeling, cooperative governance legal counsel | $25,000 | $10,000 (Co-op Partner) | $35,000 |\n| **Total** | | **$250,000** | **$125,000** | **$375,000** |\n\n## 5. Key Performance Deliverables\n- 1 resident-owned cooperative grocery storefront operational in Eastern Crescent.\n- 51%+ board seats held by low-income resident-owners.\n- 30%+ farm inventory sourced from local regional growers.\n- 2,500 low-income households served annually.\n",
